---
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/layout/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { CITATIONS, citationList } from '../data/citations';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Workout and Calculator Methodology"
  description="See how WorkoutMatch organizes balanced sessions, applies constraints, calculates adult BMI, BMR, and TDEE estimates, and documents its source limitations."
  path="/methodology/"
>
  <div class="container">
    <Breadcrumbs
      items={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Methodology' }]}
    />
  </div>

  <header class="narrow section-sm">
    <p class="eyebrow">How WorkoutMatch works</p>
    <h1>Methodology</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      WorkoutMatch turns explicit user constraints into general exercise
      sessions and shows the formulas behind its adult calculators. This page
      explains those rules, their sources, and where an estimate or template
      stops being useful.
    </p>
  </header>

  <article class="narrow methodology">
    <section aria-labelledby="workout-method-heading">
      <h2 id="workout-method-heading">How workouts are assembled</h2>
      <p>
        The generator is rule-based. It first validates duration, experience,
        goal, focus, available equipment, space, noise, impact, position, and
        any selected movement preferences. It removes exercises that conflict
        with those inputs before filling a duration- and focus-specific
        template.
      </p>
      <p>
        For a full-body session with enough time, the template tries to cover
        knee-dominant, hip-dominant, push, pull, and trunk or carry patterns.
        Short or focused sessions use reduced templates rather than pretending
        every pattern fits. Candidate exercises are scored together to avoid
        duplicates and excessive repetition of one primary area. Warm-up and
        cooldown time are part of the requested duration.
      </p>

      <h3>Constraints remain binding</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          One-dumbbell availability cannot fill a movement that simultaneously
          requires two dumbbells.
        </li>
        <li>
          Standing-only and no-floor selections remove non-standing exercise
          positions.
        </li>
        <li>
          No-jumping excludes high-impact jumping movements; quiet removes
          exercises classified as loud.
        </li>
        <li>Beginner mode excludes advanced-only entries.</li>
        <li>
          If the remaining set cannot fill a coherent template, the result
          explains the conflict instead of silently ignoring it.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout">
        <h3>What “balanced” means here</h3>
        <p>
          Balanced means the chosen template covers complementary movement
          patterns within the stated constraints. It does not mean medically
          balanced, personalized to anatomy, or optimal for every person, sport,
          goal, or week of training.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="activity-context-heading">
      <h2 id="activity-context-heading">Public-health activity context</h2>
      <p>
        The <a href={CITATIONS.whoActivity.url}
          >World Health Organization physical-activity source</a
        >
        describes physical activity broadly as movement that requires energy expenditure.
        It says all physical activity counts, any amount is better than none, and
        muscle strengthening benefits everyone. Its guidelines are population-level
        public-health recommendations covering frequency, intensity, and duration—not
        a personalized prescription from this site.
      </p>
      <p>
        For adults, WHO recommends 150–300 minutes of moderate-intensity aerobic
        activity, 75–150 minutes of vigorous-intensity aerobic activity, or an
        equivalent combination across the week. It also recommends
        muscle-strengthening activity involving the major muscle groups on two
        or more days a week. That weekly public-health context should not be
        read as a requirement to begin at the full amount or to make every
        WorkoutMatch session vigorous. A beginner may choose a smaller,
        manageable amount and build gradually when appropriate.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="calculator-method-heading">
      <h2 id="calculator-method-heading">Calculator formulas</h2>

      <h3>Adult body mass index</h3>
      <p>
        BMI is calculated as weight in kilograms divided by height in metres
        squared. The
        <a href={CITATIONS.cdcBmiOverview.url}>CDC BMI overview</a> describes it as
        a screening measure of weight relative to height. It does not directly measure
        body fat and does not distinguish fat, muscle, and bone mass or show where
        fat is carried. An individual interpretation needs other information; BMI
        is not a diagnosis.
      </p>
      <p>
        For adults aged 20 and older, WorkoutMatch follows the
        <a href={CITATIONS.cdcBmiCategories.url}>CDC adult categories</a>: below
        18.5, 18.5 to below 25, 25 to below 30, and 30 or above. The site does
        not apply these adult thresholds to children or teens.
      </p>

      <h3>Mifflin–St Jeor resting-energy estimate</h3>
      <p>
        The BMR tool uses the sex-specific simplified equations reported in the
        <a href={CITATIONS.mifflinPaper.url}
          >original Mifflin and colleagues publication</a
        >. With weight in kilograms, height in centimetres, and age in years,
        the equations used are:
      </p>
      <div
        class="formula-card card card-pad"
        aria-label="Mifflin–St Jeor equations"
      >
        <p><strong>Male equation:</strong> 10 × kg + 6.25 × cm − 5 × age + 5</p>
        <p>
          <strong>Female equation:</strong> 10 × kg + 6.25 × cm − 5 × age − 161
        </p>
      </div>
      <p>
        The 1990 paper derived a predictive equation from 498 healthy subjects
        aged 19–78 whose resting energy expenditure was measured by indirect
        calorimetry. A calculated result remains a prediction, not a metabolic
        measurement. The sex input is required by this specific published
        equation and is not a complete statement of gender identity; the tool
        can show both estimates.
      </p>

      <h3>Total daily energy expenditure estimate</h3>
      <p>
        TDEE multiplies a Mifflin–St Jeor estimate by the selected activity
        factor. Those categories are centralized heuristics, and selecting one
        is subjective. The tool displays the exact factor and a range based on
        neighbouring categories where available. Its −10%, maintenance, and +10%
        values are arithmetic comparisons, not dietary recommendations or
        guaranteed weight-change plans.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="limits-heading">
      <h2 id="limits-heading">Limitations and review boundaries</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          The generator cannot observe technique, symptoms, readiness, fatigue,
          or the safety of a room or piece of equipment.
        </li>
        <li>
          Movement-avoidance tags are conservative filter preferences, not
          medical contraindications or diagnoses.
        </li>
        <li>
          A single generated or curated workout is not a personalized multi-week
          program.
        </li>
        <li>
          BMI and energy equations simplify individual variation and should not
          be presented as measurements.
        </li>
        <li>
          No page is claimed to be medically or professionally reviewed unless a
          real reviewer and review record are explicitly present; no such claim
          is made here.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        See <a href="/safety/">Safety</a> for decision guidance, or use the
        <a href="/generate/">workout generator</a> after reviewing these boundaries.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="sources-heading">
      <h2 id="sources-heading">Source register</h2>
      <p>
        These links are centralized in the project so calculator and
        health-context copy can be audited. “Source checked” records when this
        implementation last checked the linked page; it is not a medical review
        date.
      </p>
      <ul class="source-list">
        {
          citationList.map((source) => (
            <li class="card card-pad" id={source.id}>
              <a href={source.url}>{source.title}</a>
              <span>{source.publisher}</span>
              <small>Source checked {source.reviewedOn}</small>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .methodology {
    padding-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  .methodology > section + section {
    margin-top: clamp(3rem, 7vw, 5rem);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    padding-top: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.5rem);
  }

  .methodology p,
  .methodology li {
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
  }

  .methodology li + li {
    margin-top: 0.55rem;
  }

  .callout,
  .formula-card {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .callout h3 {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .formula-card p {
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .formula-card p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .source-list {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    margin: 1.5rem 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .source-list li {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.25rem;
  }

  .source-list a {
    font-weight: 780;
  }

  .source-list small {
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
  }
</style>
